= Acknowledgements <acknowledgements>

I would like to thank my supervisors, Alexandra Graß and Helmut Seidl, for
their guidance and support throughout this thesis. I am especially grateful to
Alexandra for the many discussions, detailed feedback, and ideas that shaped
the project. I greatly appreciated the freedom to explore the topic in depth
and to follow directions that emerged along the way. I also thank Javier Esparza for
agreeing to examine this thesis.

I am grateful to Michael Petter for helping to scope the project and shape
its initial direction, and to Julian Erhard for ideas and feedback that helped
align the formalization with Goblint's design. I thank Sarah Tilscher for developing
and maintaining the verified TD solver formalization on which this work builds.

I would also like to thank Tobias Nipkow for his courses _Semantics of Programming
Languages_ and _Functional Data Structures_, Helmut Seidl for _Program Optimization_,
and Michael Petter and Julian Erhard for the practical course _Static Analysis:
Automated Bug Hunting and Beyond_. These courses sparked my interest in formal
verification and static analysis and ultimately led me to the topic of this thesis.

I am grateful to the members of the Chair for Programming Languages, Compiler
Construction and Specification Formalisms. My connection to the chair began
with the course _Functional Programming and Verification_, and it became an
important part of my time at TUM. Working there twice as a teaching assistant
and once as a tutor gave me insight into both teaching and research, and showed
me how much I enjoy teaching formal methods.

I also acknowledge the many contributors to Goblint, whose work provided both
a practical reference point and much of the inspiration for this thesis, as well
as the developers of Isabelle and its surrounding open-source tooling. In particular,
the Isabelle interfaces provided by AutoCorrode and Kevin Kappelmann's MCP server
made it practical to integrate AI-assisted development into this project.

Finally, I would like to thank my family and friends. My family gave me the
freedom to focus fully on my work and encouraged me through every stage of my
studies. My friends provided welcome distractions and regular reminders that
there is a world outside Isabelle, and their company made the more intense
periods of work much easier. Their patience and friendship have meant a great
deal to me throughout my time at TUM.
